1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,707
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,768 --> 00:00:10,146
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:10,146 --> 00:00:10,878
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,939 --> 00:00:13,585
Welcome to Unit 9: Density.

5
00:00:14,646 --> 00:00:19,281
An iceberg is a floating mass of freshwater ice, sometimes 100 metres long,

6
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:21,720
and most of it hides below the surface.

7
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:26,963
As the planet warms, more icebergs break off the polar ice shelves. Why do they float?

8
00:00:28,024 --> 00:00:30,585
To find density, we need volume and mass.

9
00:00:30,585 --> 00:00:35,524
For a regular solid like a cuboid, volume equals length times width times height.

10
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:40,366
For an irregular object, use Archimedes' displacement method.

11
00:00:40,366 --> 00:00:43,842
Read the water level, add the object, and read it again.

12
00:00:43,842 --> 00:00:49,512
If the water rises from 50 to 60 cubic centimetres, the key's volume is 10 cubic centimetres.

13
00:00:50,573 --> 00:00:52,342
Measure mass with a balance.

14
00:00:52,342 --> 00:00:57,586
For a liquid, weigh the empty container, then the container with liquid, and subtract.

15
00:00:58,647 --> 00:01:02,488
A box of stones is much heavier than the same box of feathers.

16
00:01:02,488 --> 00:01:06,695
Stone has a higher density: more mass is packed into the same volume.

17
00:01:07,756 --> 00:01:12,147
Density is mass per unit volume: density equals mass divided by volume,

18
00:01:12,147 --> 00:01:16,354
measured in grams per cubic centimetre or kilograms per cubic metre.

19
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:23,061
A metal cube of 1 cubic centimetre with a mass of 11.3 grams has a density of 11.3 grams per cubic centimetre.

20
00:01:24,122 --> 00:01:29,915
Measurements must be accurate, close to the true value, and precise, consistent when repeated.

21
00:01:29,915 --> 00:01:34,732
Industries use density to identify substances and check that products are pure.

22
00:01:35,793 --> 00:01:40,732
A class measures the volume and mass of four aluminium blocks of different sizes.

23
00:01:41,793 --> 00:01:46,183
The block with a volume of 10 cubic centimetres has a mass of 27 grams.

24
00:01:46,183 --> 00:01:49,415
The 20 cubic centimetre block has a mass of 54 grams.

25
00:01:50,476 --> 00:01:56,025
The points lie on a straight line through the origin: doubling the volume doubles the mass.

26
00:01:57,086 --> 00:02:00,256
Pause and decide: what is the density of aluminium,

27
00:02:00,256 --> 00:02:03,915
and would a 50 cubic centimetre block have the same density?
